\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Corollary}
\title{Accretive compression and the retained operator}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{For the \vocabk{massive graded Wilson data}{fdip-00IN}, choose any orthogonal decomposition #{\mathcal V=R\oplus E} and the maps #{A,B,C,D,S} of \ref{fdip-00IX}. Both #{D} and #{S} are \vocabk{strictly accretive}{fdip-009K} with lower bound #{m}, and hence invertible. If #{R,E} are invariant under #{\Gamma}, their inherited gradings satisfy
##{D^{\dagger}=\Gamma_E D\Gamma_E,\qquad S^{\dagger}=\Gamma_R S\Gamma_R,\qquad
\det_E D>0,\quad\det_R S>0.}}

\proof{
\p{The compression argument in \ref{fdip-009K} retains the same quantitative lower bound. For #{e\in E}, orthogonality gives #{\operatorname{Re}\langle e,De\rangle=\operatorname{Re}\langle i_Ee,K_Ui_Ee\rangle\ge m\|e\|^2}. The lower norm argument in \ref{fdip-00IP} proves invertibility. For #{x\in R} put #{v=i_Rx-i_ED^{-1}Cx}. Then #{K_Uv=i_RSx}, so
##{\operatorname{Re}\langle x,Sx\rangle=\operatorname{Re}\langle v,K_Uv\rangle
\ge m\|v\|^2\ge m\|x\|^2.}
If the projections commute with #{\Gamma}, compressing #{K_U^{\dagger}=\Gamma K_U\Gamma} gives the displayed relation for #{D}. Taking adjoints in #{S=A-BD^{-1}C} gives its relation for #{S}. Compressing the accretive homotopy of \ref{fdip-00IP} proves #{\det D>0}. Applying #{\int\extd\bar\psi_R\,\extd\psi_R} to the zero-source partial formula gives #{\det K_U=\det D\det S}, hence #{\det S>0}. The corresponding determinant factorization in a chosen basis is supported by \leanref{Matrix.det_fromBlocks₂₂}; that declaration does not establish the coefficient integral or its source extension.}
}
